CURRENT V6 FEATURES

1. Video upload and playback

The application accepts MP4 video uploads, stores video metadata, and supports range-based playback through the video API.

Current upload limitations remain approximately:

- MP4 only
- 100 MB upload limit
- Short video excerpts are the practical testing workflow

2. Manual player and ball labeling

Users can:

- Pause the video.
- Select a roster player.
- Draw a box around that player.
- Draw a ball box.
- Correct an existing box.
- Assign team and player identity.
- Add substitutes.
- Record substitutions.
- Move through the video frame by frame.
- Review and remove incorrect observations.

3. Automatic detection

The automatic detector uses:

- YOLOX-Tiny
- COCO-style person and sports-ball classes
- ONNX Runtime Web
- Browser-side inference
- Tiled image scanning for distant players
- Optional detailed scanning
- Pitch/grass filtering
- Focused crops for local ball searches

The detector is generic. It is not trained specifically for:

- Soccer broadcast footage
- Individual player identity
- Jersey numbers
- Goalkeeper recognition
- Team-specific uniforms
- Soccer field geometry

The detector may find:

- Players
- Referees
- Coaches
- Sideline staff
- Spectators near the pitch
- False ball candidates
- Small bright objects

Team assignment and identity still require user confirmation.

4. Player tracking

Player tracking includes:

- Persistent tracks
- One-to-one global assignment
- Appearance matching
- Shirt color/hue/brightness features
- Camera translation and scale estimation
- Short motion predictions
- Scene-cut detection
- Substitution-aware active rosters
- Confidence-based matching
- Ambiguity rejection

If the tracker is uncertain, it can:

- Preserve a short estimated path
- Mark an observation as predicted
- Stop assigning the identity
- Add a review issue
- Ask the user to confirm a later frame

The design intentionally prefers a missing observation over silently switching a player’s identity.

5. Players missing from the starting frame

A major V6 feature is partial starting-frame support.

The user does not need all 22 players to be visible at kickoff.

A player can be:

- Present in the roster but not visible at the start
- First labeled later when they enter the camera view
- Added by drawing a box manually
- Assigned from a detector candidate
- Connected to their existing roster identity

The tracker stores player appearance memory and historical labels so that an off-screen player can be remembered.

6. Off-screen player memory

When a player leaves the camera view, the application attempts to remember:

- Their player ID
- Last known box
- Appearance signature
- Last-seen time
- Approximate edge/location context
- Team assignment

A player who disappears is not automatically removed from the match.

When that player returns:

- Appearance must be compatible.
- Location/motion history may be used.
- Multiple observations are required.
- The candidate must not conflict with another active player.
- Ambiguous returns remain unresolved.
- Recovered identities are marked as inferred/reidentified and should be reviewed.

Long absences are deliberately conservative. Same-kit teammates should not be reassigned merely because the roster has one remaining name.

A uniquely dressed goalkeeper or player may be easier to recover than a same-kit outfield player.

7. Roster deduction

The project includes a conservative “remaining teammate” suggestion.

It only proposes a roster identity when:

- Exactly one roster identity remains unassigned for that team.
- Ten confirmed teammates are available.
- Enough opposing-team appearance examples exist.
- A unique visual candidate matches the team appearance.
- The candidate is not equally compatible with another identity.

Roster count alone must never silently assign a player.

8. Ball tracking

The ball has a separate tracking path from player tracking.

It uses:

- Motion prediction
- Camera-adjusted position
- Ball size/diameter continuity
- Compact bright-component detection
- Shape and aspect-ratio checks
- Local contrast
- Long-line rejection
- Nearby-player context
- Last confirmed ball position
- The area near the feet of the last nearby player
- A focused neural detector crop when confidence falls

When the ball is lost, the tracker can search around:

- The predicted ball position
- The last confirmed ball position
- The last player associated with the ball’s vicinity

Nearby-player context is only a search hint. It does not assume that the player possesses the ball.

The ball tracker is improved but not solved. It can still confuse:

- White boots
- Field markings
- Bright advertising
- Small objects
- Occluded ball positions
- A ball close to a player’s feet

After prolonged uncertainty, automatic mode pauses and asks the user to confirm the ball.

9. Field references

Field references can be added over multiple frames.

The user can mark:

- Far-left corner
- Far-right corner
- Near-right corner
- Near-left corner
- Far touchline
- Near touchline
- Left goal line
- Right goal line

Corners and boundary-line segments do not need to be visible simultaneously.

Each field reference stores:

- Landmark name
- Timestamp
- One point for a corner
- Two points for a line
- Camera-segment context

During tracking, image-plane camera estimates can project a reference to later frames while the camera motion remains reliable.

These projections are estimates. The field system is not yet:

- A homography
- A true pitch calibration system
- A metric coordinate system
- An automatic field-line detector
- A system that can infer all unseen corners from one line

Camera cuts or unreliable motion estimates stop field projection.

10. Crowded visual interface

The V6 interface changed candidate labels so that:

- Name banners are hidden by default.
- Labels appear on hover.
- Labels appear when a box is selected.
- Keyboard focus reveals the label.
- Touch users can select a box.
- “Show all name labels” restores the old full-label view.
- Thin outlines remain visible.
- Field mode suppresses normal candidate selection.

11. Automatic processing

The user can choose:

- Five-second tracking
- Fifteen-second tracking
- Auto until review

Automatic mode:

- Processes player detections at approximately 5 Hz.
- Processes ball tracking at approximately 10 Hz.
- Uses checkpoints approximately every five seconds.
- Saves observations to the tracking document.
- Pauses on critical uncertainty.
- Preserves unsaved local state if a save fails.
- Supports JSON export.
- Allows returning to the saved checkpoint.

The application still processes in the foreground browser. Closing the tab stops processing.

12. Review and evidence labels

Saved points may be labeled as:

- Manual
- Detector
- Experimental
- Detection evidence
- Predicted evidence
- Appearance evidence
- Reidentified evidence

Estimated and inferred observations are visually distinguished.

Users are not required to approve every frame, but unresolved issues and inferred identities should be reviewed.

