KNOWN LIMITATIONS

1. Processing is very slow

The current detector runs in the browser using CPU/WASM inference. A five-second clip may take approximately two minutes to process.

A full 90-minute match is not currently practical.

Likely performance improvements:

- Use WebGPU where supported.
- Reduce detector frequency.
- Track between detector calls.
- Scan only predicted regions.
- Avoid repeated full/tiled scans.
- Move inference into a Web Worker.
- Use a faster/smaller model.
- Use native/server-side ONNX inference.
- Add background job processing.
- Store results incrementally in chunks.

2. Generic detection model

YOLOX-Tiny COCO is generic, not soccer-specific.

It does not understand:

- Jersey numbers
- Individual player identity
- Team tactics
- Soccer formations
- Soccer events
- Possession
- Passes
- Tackles
- Shots
- Fouls
- Offside
- Goalkeeper-specific behavior

3. Identity uncertainty

Appearance matching is lightweight and based mainly on image appearance and shirt features.

It can fail when:

- Teammates wear nearly identical kits.
- Players overlap.
- Players are very small.
- The camera zooms significantly.
- Lighting changes.
- Players leave and return after long gaps.
- The viewpoint changes sharply.

4. Ball limitations

The ball is very small in most footage and may be occluded or visually similar to boots, lines, or bright objects.

5. Full-match storage

The current document storage is still bounded.

The runtime uses a conservative observation cap to avoid oversized API JSON documents. Full-match chunked storage has not been implemented.

6. No background processing

Processing stops when the browser tab closes or the computer sleeps.

7. No production GPU pipeline

The system does not yet have:

- Server-side GPU workers
- Job queue
- Progress persistence independent of the browser
- Automatic retrying
- Full-match resumability
- Distributed processing

8. No analytics engine yet

The current app mainly creates and reviews tracking observations.

It does not yet calculate reliable:

- Formations
- Heatmaps
- Distance covered
- Speed
- Spacing
- Possession
- Pass networks
- Tactical events
- Team shape
- Player ratings
- Coaching insights
- Season-long analytics

RECOMMENDED NEXT DEVELOPMENT ORDER

Priority 1: Performance

Before adding advanced analytics, make processing practical.

Recommended sequence:

1. Add timing instrumentation around:
   - Video seeking
   - Canvas frame extraction
   - ONNX inference
   - Crop count
   - Ball fallback inference
   - JSON persistence

2. Add WebGPU detection and an execution-provider fallback:
   - Try WebGPU.
   - Fall back to WASM.
   - Clearly show the active backend and measured speed.

3. Move inference into a Web Worker so the UI remains responsive.

4. Reduce full detector scans:
   - Initial scan: comprehensive.
   - Normal tracking: use lightweight tracker.
   - Detector scan only every N frames or when confidence drops.
   - Use local crops around predicted player positions.
   - Use full scans after camera cuts or when many identities disappear.

5. Benchmark:
   - Five-second clip processing time.
   - Per-frame detector time.
   - Player continuity.
   - Ball continuity.
   - False identity switches.
   - Memory use.

Priority 2: Full-match data architecture

Replace one large JSON tracking document with chunked storage.

Potential design:

- Match metadata record.
- Tracking chunks by time range, such as 30 seconds or 60 seconds.
- One chunk per player/time range or time block.
- Checkpoint record.
- Event/issue table.
- Revision/version metadata.

Do not simply raise the JSON size limit. Large single documents will eventually become unreliable.

Priority 3: Background job processing

Create a server-side job workflow:

1. User uploads the video.
2. User labels initial players.
3. Server creates a processing job.
4. Worker reads video frames.
5. Worker performs inference and tracking.
6. Results are saved incrementally.
7. UI polls or subscribes to progress.
8. User reviews unresolved intervals.

This will allow full-match processing even if the browser closes.

Priority 4: Better soccer-specific perception

Evaluate a soccer-trained detector or tracking model.

Potential improvements:

- Soccer player/ball detector.
- Jersey-number OCR.
- Team-color classifier.
- Pose/keypoint features.
- Re-identification embeddings.
- Goalkeeper-specific appearance.
- Field-line detector.
- Camera calibration model.

Do not replace the current generic model without benchmarking false positives, distant players, and ball recall.

Priority 5: Field calibration

Extend field references into:

- Automatic line detection.
- Homography estimation.
- Metric pitch coordinates.
- Camera pan/zoom compensation.
- Player locations in real field coordinates.
- Distance and speed calculations.

The current field system should be treated as an image-reference foundation, not a finished calibration system.

Priority 6: Analytics layer

Once tracking data is reliable enough, add derived analytics as separate modules:

- Team shape
- Player spacing
- Formation changes
- Width and depth
- Defensive line
- Possession zones
- Ball progression
- Pass/receive candidates
- Pressing events
- Transition events
- Player heatmaps
- Distance/speed
- Tactical review timestamps

Every analytic result should preserve uncertainty and link back to source timestamps.

DEVELOPMENT PRINCIPLES

- Do not silently assign player identities when evidence is weak.
- Missing data is preferable to incorrect identity data.
- Preserve backward compatibility when changing the tracking schema.
- Add regression tests for every tracking change.
- Keep manual correction possible at every stage.
- Treat predicted, inferred, detector, and manually confirmed observations differently.
- Do not claim improved tracking accuracy without testing actual footage.
- Benchmark both speed and identity accuracy.
- Do not include user videos or production credentials in GitHub.
- Keep the local mock authentication limited to localhost.
- Preserve third-party license files.
- Read the existing README and docs/DEVELOPER_HANDOFF.md before making major changes.
